module proteinPrecipitationDetection
module digestCompletionDetection

manifest ProteinSample
manifest TrichloroaceticAcid
manifest WashBuffer
manifest ResolubilizingBuffer
manifest TCEP_Reductant
manifest Iodoacetamide
manifest Trypsin

// ============================================================
// Digital Microfluidics for Automated Proteomic Processing
// Jebrail M.J., Luk V.N., Shih S.C.C., Fobel R., Ng A.H.C.,
// Yang H., Freire S.L.S., Wheeler A.R.
// JoVE. 33. DOI: 10.3791/1603 (2009)
//
// Protocol integrates protein extraction by precipitation with
// multistep solution-phase processing: reduction, alkylation,
// and tryptic digestion -- all on a single DMF device.
//
// DEVICE CONFIGURATION:
//   Two-plate DMF device (bottom: patterned Cr electrodes,
//   Parylene-C dielectric, Teflon-AF hydrophobic layer;
//   top: unpatterned ITO with Teflon-AF).
//   Gap set by 140 um double-sided tape.
//   Actuation: 100 V_rms / 18 kHz via 40-pin connectors.
//   Droplet volume: ~600 nL (defined by electrode dimensions).
//   Control: LabView (National Instruments) with in-house
//   feedback calibration.
//
// RESERVOIR MAP (Figure 1b):
//   R-1  ProteinSample       -- serum/protein in working buffer
//   R-2  TrichloroaceticAcid -- 20% TCA precipitant in DI water
//   R-3  Waste reservoir     -- supernatant / waste (modelled as drain)
//   R-4  WashBuffer          -- 70/30 Chloroform/ACN rinse
//   R-5  ResolubilizingBuffer-- 100 mM AmBic pH 8.0, 0.08% Pluronic
//   R-6  TCEP_Reductant      -- 10 mM TCEP in working buffer
//   R-7  Iodoacetamide       -- 12 mM IAM in working buffer
//   R-8  Trypsin             -- trypsin at 1/5 of protein conc.
//
// WORKING BUFFER (WB): 100 mM TrisHCl pH 7.8, 0.08% Pluronic F127.
//   Pluronic F127 is a surfactant additive to prevent biofouling.
//
// NOTE ON PRECIPITATION ABSTRACTION:
//   Steps 2-4 use mix/heat to abstract the DMF precipitation
//   event (protein:TCA merge, incubation, supernatant removal,
//   and wash). BioScript has no native "precipitate" or "wash"
//   primitive; mix + heat model the electrode-actuated incubation
//   and the wash steps are modelled as sequential mix/drain.
//   The precipitate physically remains on the device surface
//   between wash and resolubilization; this is abstracted by
//   allowing the variable to persist until disposeWaste.
//
// NOTE ON MIXING PATTERN:
//   Parts 4.5-4.7 specify circular mixing (6-electrode circular
//   actuation) for reduction, alkylation, and digestion steps.
//   All mix durations reflect actual incubation times from the
//   protocol (not mixing time only). BioScript `mix` unifies
//   both the droplet merging+mixing actuation and the subsequent
//   incubation.
//
// NOTE ON TEMPERATURE CONTROL:
//   heat at 25c models room-temperature incubation steps.
//   heat at 37c models the hot plate used for tryptic digestion.
//   BioScript duration tokens are s / m only.
//   Incubation times: reduction 1h = 60m, alkylation 15m,
//   digestion 3h = 180m.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                -- extraction, reduction, alkylation,
//                               digestion, post-processing
//   BB2  digestPass          -- digestSignal > 0: digestion complete;
//                               dispose processed sample
//   BB3  digestFail          -- digestSignal <= 0: incomplete digest;
//                               drain sample
//   BB4  finalCleanup        -- terminal: dispose QC signal
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 3 / PART 4 - STEP 1: Dispense protein sample droplet
//   from R-1. Protein dissolved in WB (100 mM TrisHCl pH 7.8,
//   0.08% Pluronic F127). ~600 nL dispensed by electrode
//   actuation. This is the starting material for the entire
//   proteomic workup (e.g. BSA as model protein, Figure 4).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

proteinDroplet     = dispense 1 units of ProteinSample

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 2a: Dispense precipitant from R-2.
//   20% Trichloroacetic acid (TCA) in DI water. TCA
//   denatures and precipitates proteins by lowering pH and
//   disrupting hydration shells.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

precipitantDroplet = dispense 1 units of TrichloroaceticAcid

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 2b: Merge protein + precipitant droplets.
//   Combined droplet incubated for 5 min at room temperature.
//   Proteins precipitate onto the device surface (Figure 2,
//   Frames 1-3). `mix` abstracts the electrode-driven merge
//   and 5-min incubation on the DMF surface.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

precipitationMix   = mix proteinDroplet with precipitantDroplet for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 2c: Remove supernatant (actuate away to R-3).
//   After precipitation, the aqueous supernatant (containing
//   TCA and soluble contaminants) is actuated to the waste
//   reservoir. The protein precipitate remains on-surface.
//   `drain` models the electrode-driven supernatant removal
//   to R-3 (Figure 2, Frame 4).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

drain precipitationMix

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 3: Wash precipitate with three droplets of
//   wash buffer from R-4 (70/30 Chloroform/ACN).
//   Each droplet is driven across the precipitated protein and
//   actuated to waste reservoir R-3 to remove residual TCA
//   and non-protein contaminants (Figure 2, Frame 5).
//   Three sequential wash steps modelled as independent
//   dispense/drain pairs (see NOTE ON COMPILER CONSTRAINT).
//
//   NOTE ON COMPILER CONSTRAINT: lilott8/BioScript does not
//   support loop constructs or multi-unit wash iteration.
//   The three wash steps are unrolled explicitly. Each wash
//   droplet is dispensed independently and drained individually.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wash1              = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
drain wash1

wash2              = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
drain wash2

wash3              = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
drain wash3

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 4: Resolubilize protein precipitate.
//   Allow precipitate to dry on surface, then dispense
//   resolubilizing buffer from R-5 (100 mM Ammonium Bicarbonate
//   pH 8.0, 0.08% Pluronic F127). Incubate 20 min until
//   precipitate fully dissolves (Figure 2, Frame 6).
//   `mix` with heat at 25c abstracts the 20-min incubation
//   at room temperature.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

resolubilizingBuffer = dispense 1 units of ResolubilizingBuffer

// Dispense a second protein droplet to represent the
// on-surface precipitate being re-contacted with buffer.
// This models the resolubilization of the dried protein
// precipitate on the electrode surface into the buffer droplet.
proteinPrecipitate   = dispense 1 units of ProteinSample

resolubilizedSample  = mix proteinPrecipitate with resolubilizingBuffer for 20m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 5: Reduction.
//   Dispense reductant (TCEP) from R-6 and merge with sample.
//   TCEP (tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine) at 10 mM reduces
//   disulfide bonds. Mix by circular 6-electrode actuation.
//   Incubate 1 h (60 min) at room temperature in a humidified
//   chamber (Figure 3, Frames 1-2).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

reductantDroplet   = dispense 1 units of TCEP_Reductant
reducedSample      = mix resolubilizedSample with reductantDroplet for 60m
heat reducedSample at 25c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 6: Alkylation.
//   Dispense alkylating agent (IAM) from R-7 and merge with
//   sample. Iodoacetamide (12 mM) alkylates free thiols to
//   prevent re-oxidation of cysteines. Mix as in step 5.
//   Incubate 15 min at room temperature, protected from light
//   (Figure 3, Frames 2-3).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

alkylatingDroplet  = dispense 1 units of Iodoacetamide
alkylatedSample    = mix reducedSample with alkylatingDroplet for 15m
heat alkylatedSample at 25c for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - STEP 7: Tryptic digestion.
//   Dispense trypsin from R-8 and merge with sample. Trypsin
//   concentration is 1/5 of total protein concentration.
//   Mix as in step 5 (circular 6-electrode actuation).
//   Incubate 3 h (180 min) at 37 C in a humidified chamber
//   on a hot plate (Figure 3, Frames 3-4).
//   Trypsin cleaves at Lys-X and Arg-X peptide bonds.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

trypsinDroplet     = dispense 1 units of Trypsin
digestedSample     = mix alkylatedSample with trypsinDroplet for 180m
heat digestedSample at 37c for 180m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 4 - POST-PROCESSING QC:
//   Detect digest completion via peptide signal (abstracts the
//   downstream nano-HPLC/MS readout described in Parts 5-6).
//   In the paper, the processed sample is quenched with 0.6 mL
//   2.5% TFA (off-chip), purified by C18 ZipTip SPE, and
//   evaluated by nano-HPLC-MS/MS. For the eGFP-analogue here,
//   digestCompletionDetection abstracts the SEQUEST database
//   search result (protein ID at 99.9% confidence, >=30%
//   sequence coverage; Figure 4, 25 peptides from BSA, 44% cov).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

digestSignal = detect digestCompletionDetection on digestedSample for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Digest QC gate
// Pass (BB2): digestSignal > 0 -> digestion complete and
//             peptides detected at required confidence;
//             dispose processed sample.
// Fail (BB3): digestSignal <= 0 -> digest incomplete or no
//             identifiable peptides; drain sample.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (digestSignal > 0) {
    dispose digestedSample
} else {
    drain digestedSample
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose digest QC signal droplet. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose digestSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
